#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""eh-fake-bridge -- a scriptable stand-in for a package's backend process.

DESIGN.md Section 9.2.  A large class of backend-talking behaviour is
impossible or miserable to trigger against a real backend: a half-open
connection, a request that never gets answered, a reply truncated
mid-JSON, a backend that dies in the middle of writing.  This script
speaks a line-oriented JSON protocol on stdin/stdout and does whatever a
.jsonl script tells it to, on demand and to the byte.

It knows nothing about any particular package.  What it knows is the
*shape* almost every such protocol shares:

  * a request is one JSON object per line, carrying a correlation id;
  * a reply is one JSON object per line, carrying the same id;
  * some traffic is out-of-band -- incremental output attached to a
    request still in flight, or an unsolicited asynchronous event.

Which JSON *keys* carry those three things differs per package, so they
are configurable (--id-key / --method-key / --params-key, and the
`emit' item keys in the script).  Everything else is generic.

Usage
-----

    eh-fake-bridge --script FILE.jsonl [--script MORE.jsonl ...]
                   [--fault NAME] [--log FILE]
                   [--id-key id] [--method-key method] [--params-key params]
                   [--unmatched error|empty|never]

    eh-fake-bridge --record OUT.jsonl -- REAL-BACKEND-COMMAND [ARGS...]

Any other command-line argument is ignored, deliberately: a profile
points the package's own "how do I launch my backend" option at this
script, and the package will append its own flags (a URL, a token, a
timeout) that it expects its real backend to understand.  Ignoring them
is what lets the substitution be invisible to the package under test.

Script format
-------------

One JSON object per line.  Blank lines, and lines whose first
non-whitespace character is `#' or `//', are ignored.  Two kinds of
object:

**Rules** match an incoming request.  Checked in file order (across all
--script files, concatenated); first match wins.

    {"on":     {"method": "execute", "code": "~^for i in~"},
     "delay_ms": 50,
     "emit":  [{"after_ms":   0, "event":  {"type":"status","execution_state":"busy"}},
               {"after_ms": 100, "output": {"type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"step 1\n"}},
               {"after_ms": 310, "output": {"type":"display_data",
                                            "data":{"image/png":{"$file":"plot.png"}}}}],
     "reply": {"outputs": "<repeat-all-emitted>", "execution_count": 1}}

  `on'        every key must match the request.  `method' is matched
              against the request's method; every other key is matched
              against that key in the request's params.  A value wrapped
              in tildes (`"~^for i in~"') is a regular expression
              searched against the value as a string; anything else must
              be equal.  `{"on": {}}' matches every request -- useful as
              a catch-all last rule.
  `reply'     becomes the reply's `result'.  The string
              "<repeat-all-emitted>", used as a value or as a list
              element, expands to the list of output payloads this rule
              emitted for this request -- real backends routinely repeat
              streamed chunks in the final combined reply, and getting
              that reconciliation wrong is a real bug class.
  `error'     replies with an `error' object instead of a result.
  `delay_ms'  wait this long before replying (after the emits).
  `emit'      out-of-band traffic, each item scheduled `after_ms' from
              when the request arrived.  An item carries exactly one of
              `output' (attached to this request's id), `event' (no id;
              a top-level `kernel_id'-style routing key may sit beside
              it) or `input_request' (attached to this request's id;
              the package is expected to answer on stdin).
  `never_reply'  emit as scripted, then leave the request hanging
              forever -- the wedged-request case.
  `fault'     apply a fault (see below) to this rule's reply.
  `times'     how many times this rule may match before it is retired
              and later rules get a look in; default unlimited.

A rule may match on `on_input' instead of `on'.  Those match a line the
client sent that is *not* a request -- the answer to an
`input_request', typically -- against the line's own top-level keys:

    {"on_input": {"input": "~.~"},
     "emit": [{"after_ms": 0, "output": {"type":"stream","text":"got it\n"}}]}

Correlation is by id alone on such a line, so without this the fake
could pose a question and never react to the answer, leaving the
interesting half of that exchange untestable.  An `on_input' rule with
no `reply' emits and stops, rather than inventing a reply line no real
backend would send.

**Injections** fire on a wall-clock schedule from bridge start,
independent of any request -- the "something else killed the backend"
case:

    {"at": "t+2000", "inject": {"event": {"type":"dead"}, "kernel_id":"k1"}}

Anywhere in an emitted or injected payload, `{"$file": "PATH"}' is
replaced by the file's contents: base64 for anything that isn't valid
UTF-8 (an image), the text itself otherwise.  PATH resolves relative to
the script file that named it.

Faults
------

`--fault NAME' applies to the first reply written; a rule's own
`"fault"' key applies to that rule's reply and takes precedence.

  truncate-json   write the first half of the reply, then a newline
  no-newline      write the reply with no trailing newline
  slow-drip       write the reply one byte at a time, 20ms apart
  die-mid-reply   write half the reply and exit(1) immediately
  stderr-noise    (global only) chatter on stderr throughout, to prove
                  the package keeps the backend's stderr out of the
                  protocol on stdout
  invalid-utf8    write a reply containing a raw invalid UTF-8 byte
  garbage-line    write a non-JSON line before the reply

Record mode
-----------

    eh-fake-bridge --record out.jsonl -- python3 -m realbackend --url ...

Proxies stdin/stdout to the real backend, passing everything through
unchanged, and writes a replayable script of what actually crossed the
wire, with binary payloads spilled to `out.jsonl.d/'.  Every fixture
should start as real traffic and be hand-edited afterwards; fixtures
built from a protocol spec are how a suite ends up passing against a
fiction.
"""

import argparse
import base64
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import threading
import time

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# output side: one lock around stdout, because emits are scheduled on
# timers and a reply can be written from the reader thread at the same
# moment.  Interleaved bytes would be a bug in the harness masquerading
# as a bug in the package under test.

class Writer:
    def __init__(self, stream, log=None):
        self._stream = stream
        self._lock = threading.Lock()
        self._log = log
        self._replies_written = 0

    def line(self, obj):
        self.raw(json.dumps(obj, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")

    def raw(self, text, encode=True):
        data = text.encode("utf-8") if encode else text
        with self._lock:
            self._stream.buffer.write(data)
            self._stream.buffer.flush()
        self.log(("-> %s" % text.rstrip("\n")) if encode else "-> <raw bytes>")

    def reply(self, obj, fault=None):
        """Write a reply line, mangled by FAULT if one is in force."""
        text = json.dumps(obj, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n"
        self._replies_written += 1

        if fault == "truncate-json":
            self.raw(text[: max(1, len(text) // 2)] + "\n")
        elif fault == "no-newline":
            self.raw(text.rstrip("\n"))
        elif fault == "garbage-line":
            self.raw("this is not JSON and never was\n")
            self.raw(text)
        elif fault == "invalid-utf8":
            half = text[: max(1, len(text) // 2)].encode("utf-8")
            self.raw(half + b"\xff\xfe" + text[len(half) :].encode("utf-8"), encode=False)
        elif fault == "slow-drip":
            for ch in text:
                self.raw(ch)
                time.sleep(0.02)
        elif fault == "die-mid-reply":
            self.raw(text[: max(1, len(text) // 2)])
            self.log("!! die-mid-reply: exiting")
            os._exit(1)
        else:
            self.raw(text)

    def replies_written(self):
        with self._lock:
            return self._replies_written

    def log(self, message):
        if self._log:
            self._log.write("%.3f %s\n" % (time.time(), message))
            self._log.flush()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# script loading

class ScriptError(Exception):
    pass


def _expand_files(value, base_dir):
    """Replace every {"$file": PATH} in VALUE with the file's contents."""
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        if set(value.keys()) == {"$file"}:
            path = os.path.join(base_dir, value["$file"])
            with open(path, "rb") as fh:
                blob = fh.read()
            try:
                return blob.decode("utf-8")
            except UnicodeDecodeError:
                return base64.b64encode(blob).decode("ascii")
        return {k: _expand_files(v, base_dir) for k, v in value.items()}
    if isinstance(value, list):
        return [_expand_files(v, base_dir) for v in value]
    return value


def load_scripts(paths):
    """Return (rules, injections) read from PATHS in order.

    One object per line is the format, but an object may be spread over
    several lines: incomplete text is held and re-parsed with the next
    line appended.  A rule with half a dozen scheduled emits is
    unreadable crammed onto one line, and an unreadable fixture is one
    nobody will keep accurate."""
    rules, injections = [], []
    for path in paths:
        base_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))
        buffered, start_line, last_error = "", 0, None
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            lines = list(enumerate(fh, 1))
        for lineno, line in lines:
            stripped = line.strip()
            if not buffered and (
                not stripped or stripped.startswith("#") or stripped.startswith("//")
            ):
                continue
            if not buffered:
                start_line = lineno
            buffered = (buffered + " " + stripped).strip()
            try:
                obj = json.loads(buffered)
            except ValueError as err:
                last_error = err
                continue
            buffered, last_error = "", None
            obj = _expand_files(obj, base_dir)
            obj["__source__"] = "%s:%d" % (os.path.basename(path), start_line)
            if "inject" in obj:
                injections.append(obj)
            elif "on" in obj or "on_input" in obj:
                obj.setdefault("__matched__", 0)
                rules.append(obj)
            else:
                raise ScriptError(
                    "%s:%d: object has none of `on', `on_input' or `inject'"
                    % (path, start_line)
                )
        if buffered:
            raise ScriptError("%s:%d: unterminated JSON object: %s"
                              % (path, start_line, last_error))
    return rules, injections


def _value_matches(pattern, actual):
    if isinstance(pattern, str) and len(pattern) >= 2 and pattern.startswith("~") and pattern.endswith("~"):
        return re.search(pattern[1:-1], "" if actual is None else str(actual)) is not None
    return pattern == actual


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# replay

class Bridge:
    def __init__(self, args, rules, injections, writer):
        self.args = args
        self.rules = rules
        self.injections = injections
        self.out = writer
        self.started = time.time()
        self._timers = []
        self._handlers = []

    # -- rule selection ------------------------------------------------

    def match(self, request):
        method = request.get(self.args.method_key)
        params = request.get(self.args.params_key) or {}
        for rule in self.rules:
            if "on" not in rule:
                continue
            times = rule.get("times")
            if times is not None and rule["__matched__"] >= times:
                continue
            on = rule["on"]
            ok = True
            for key, pattern in on.items():
                actual = method if key == self.args.method_key else params.get(key)
                if not _value_matches(pattern, actual):
                    ok = False
                    break
            if ok:
                rule["__matched__"] += 1
                return rule
        return None

    def match_inbound(self, line):
        """Select an `on_input' rule for a non-request line the client sent.

        A protocol that can ask its client a question -- a prompt, a
        credential, a confirmation -- gets the answer back on stdin with
        no method of its own, correlated only by id.  Without this the
        fake could pose the question but never react to the answer, so
        the interesting half of that exchange (does what the client
        typed actually reach the backend?) would be untestable."""
        for rule in self.rules:
            if "on_input" not in rule:
                continue
            times = rule.get("times")
            if times is not None and rule["__matched__"] >= times:
                continue
            if all(_value_matches(pattern, line.get(key))
                   for key, pattern in rule["on_input"].items()):
                rule["__matched__"] += 1
                return rule
        return None

    # -- emitting ------------------------------------------------------

    def _emit(self, item, request_id):
        """Write one out-of-band payload.  Returns the output payload it
        wrote, or None -- the caller collects those for
        <repeat-all-emitted>."""
        item = {k: v for k, v in item.items() if k != "after_ms"}
        if "output" in item:
            payload = item["output"]
            line = {self.args.id_key: request_id, "output": payload}
            line.update({k: v for k, v in item.items() if k != "output"})
            self.out.line(line)
            return payload
        if "input_request" in item:
            line = {self.args.id_key: request_id, "input_request": item["input_request"]}
            self.out.line(line)
            return None
        if "event" in item:
            self.out.line(item)
            return None
        if "raw" in item:
            self.out.raw(item["raw"])
            return None
        self.out.log("!! emit item has none of output/event/input_request/raw: %r" % (item,))
        return None

    def schedule(self, item, request_id, collected):
        """Emit ITEM after its `after_ms'.  Returns the timer, or None if
        it fired inline."""
        delay = item.get("after_ms", 0) / 1000.0

        def fire():
            payload = self._emit(item, request_id)
            if payload is not None:
                collected.append(payload)

        if delay <= 0:
            fire()
            return None
        timer = threading.Timer(delay, fire)
        timer.daemon = True
        timer.start()
        self._timers.append(timer)
        return timer

    def start_injections(self):
        for injection in self.injections:
            at = injection.get("at", "t+0")
            match = re.match(r"t\+(\d+)", str(at))
            delay = int(match.group(1)) / 1000.0 if match else 0.0
            item = injection["inject"]
            timer = threading.Timer(delay, lambda i=item: self._emit(i, None))
            timer.daemon = True
            timer.start()
            self._timers.append(timer)

    # -- the reply -----------------------------------------------------

    @staticmethod
    def _expand_repeats(value, collected):
        if value == "<repeat-all-emitted>":
            return list(collected)
        if isinstance(value, list):
            out = []
            for item in value:
                if item == "<repeat-all-emitted>":
                    out.extend(collected)
                else:
                    out.append(Bridge._expand_repeats(item, collected))
            return out
        if isinstance(value, dict):
            return {k: Bridge._expand_repeats(v, collected) for k, v in value.items()}
        return value

    def handle(self, request, rule="<match>"):
        request_id = request.get(self.args.id_key)
        if rule == "<match>":
            rule = self.match(request)

        if rule is None:
            self.out.log("?? no rule for %r" % (request,))
            if self.args.unmatched == "never":
                return
            if self.args.unmatched == "empty":
                self.out.reply({self.args.id_key: request_id, "result": {}})
                return
            self.out.reply(
                {
                    self.args.id_key: request_id,
                    "error": {
                        "message": "eh-fake-bridge: no rule matched method %r"
                        % (request.get(self.args.method_key),),
                        "status": 501,
                    },
                }
            )
            return

        collected = []
        scheduled = []
        for item in rule.get("emit", []):
            timer = self.schedule(item, request_id, collected)
            if timer is not None:
                scheduled.append(timer)

        if rule.get("never_reply") or (
            "on_input" in rule and "reply" not in rule and "error" not in rule
        ):
            # An `on_input' rule that only emits has already said
            # everything it had to say: the client's answer completes no
            # request of its own, and inventing a reply for it would put
            # a line on the wire no real backend would send.
            for timer in scheduled:
                timer.join()
            self.out.log("-- %s: emitted, no reply" % rule["__source__"])
            return

        # Join the emit timers rather than sleeping for the same span.
        # Sleeping is a race the reply loses roughly half the time when
        # the last emit is scheduled at exactly the sleep duration: the
        # reply goes out first, and `<repeat-all-emitted>' expands to a
        # chunk short.  A fixture that reproduces intermittently is worse
        # than no fixture, so wait on the actual event.
        for timer in scheduled:
            timer.join()
        delay = rule.get("delay_ms", 0) / 1000.0
        if delay > 0:
            time.sleep(delay)

        fault = rule.get("fault")
        if fault is None and self.args.fault and self.out.replies_written() == 0:
            fault = self.args.fault
        if fault == "stderr-noise":
            fault = None

        if "error" in rule:
            self.out.reply({self.args.id_key: request_id, "error": rule["error"]}, fault)
        else:
            result = self._expand_repeats(rule.get("reply", {}), collected)
            self.out.reply({self.args.id_key: request_id, "result": result}, fault)

    # -- the loop ------------------------------------------------------

    def run(self):
        self.start_injections()
        for line in sys.stdin:
            line = line.strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            self.out.log("<- %s" % line)
            try:
                request = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                # Not every first line is a request: a package may send a
                # secret or a handshake on stdin before the protocol
                # starts (jsonyter's `stdin' token transport does exactly
                # that).  Swallowing it is what makes the fake usable
                # with those transports unchanged.
                self.out.log("?? non-JSON stdin line ignored")
                continue
            if not isinstance(request, dict):
                continue
            # A reply to an input_request we emitted is inbound traffic,
            # not a new request; it carries no method.
            if self.args.method_key not in request:
                rule = self.match_inbound(request)
                if rule is None:
                    self.out.log("-- inbound non-request, no on_input rule; ignored")
                    continue
                handler = threading.Thread(
                    target=self.handle, args=(request, rule), daemon=True)
                self._handlers.append(handler)
                handler.start()
                continue
            handler = threading.Thread(target=self.handle, args=(request,), daemon=True)
            self._handlers.append(handler)
            handler.start()
        self.linger()

    def linger(self):
        """Finish scheduled work after stdin closes.

        A package that has gone away closes the pipe, and the real
        backend would then exit -- but so would we, mid-emit, before a
        scripted 200ms stream chunk had fired.  Under `eh run' that is
        invisible; piping a fixture in from a shell to check what it
        does, it looks exactly like a broken script.  Wait out the
        outstanding timers instead, up to --linger-ms."""
        deadline = time.time() + self.args.linger_ms / 1000.0
        for worker in list(self._handlers) + list(self._timers):
            remaining = deadline - time.time()
            if remaining <= 0:
                break
            worker.join(remaining)


def stderr_noise():
    """Chatter on stderr forever.  A backend whose stderr is noisy is the
    normal case, not the exotic one -- Python import warnings alone are
    enough -- and a package that lets that noise reach its protocol
    parser is broken in a way only this can prove."""
    n = 0
    while True:
        n += 1
        sys.stderr.write(
            "eh-fake-bridge: warning %d: this line must never be parsed as protocol\n" % n
        )
        sys.stderr.flush()
        time.sleep(0.2)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# record mode

def record(out_path, command, args):
    """Proxy stdin/stdout to a real backend, writing a replayable script."""
    import subprocess

    spill_dir = out_path + ".d"
    os.makedirs(spill_dir, exist_ok=True)
    child = subprocess.Popen(
        command, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, bufsize=0
    )
    script = open(out_path, "w", encoding="utf-8")
    lock = threading.Lock()
    pending = {}          # request id -> {"request":…, "t0":…, "emits":[…]}
    order = []

    def spill(value):
        """Replace any long base64-looking string with a {"$file": …} ref."""
        if isinstance(value, str) and len(value) > 512:
            blob = value.encode("utf-8")
            name = hashlib.sha256(blob).hexdigest()[:16] + ".bin"
            path = os.path.join(spill_dir, name)
            if not os.path.exists(path):
                with open(path, "wb") as fh:
                    fh.write(blob)
            return {"$file": os.path.join(os.path.basename(spill_dir), name)}
        if isinstance(value, dict):
            return {k: spill(v) for k, v in value.items()}
        if isinstance(value, list):
            return [spill(v) for v in value]
        return value

    def write_rule(entry):
        request = entry["request"]
        params = request.get(args.params_key) or {}
        on = {args.method_key: request.get(args.method_key)}
        for key in ("code", "name"):
            if key in params:
                on[key] = params[key]
        rule = {"on": on}
        if entry["emits"]:
            rule["emit"] = entry["emits"]
        if "error" in entry.get("reply", {}):
            rule["error"] = spill(entry["reply"]["error"])
        else:
            rule["reply"] = spill(entry.get("reply", {}).get("result", {}))
        script.write(json.dumps(rule, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")
        script.flush()

    def pump_out():
        for raw in child.stdout:
            sys.stdout.buffer.write(raw)
            sys.stdout.buffer.flush()
            try:
                message = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8").strip())
            except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                continue
            if not isinstance(message, dict):
                continue
            with lock:
                mid = message.get(args.id_key)
                entry = pending.get(mid)
                if entry is None:
                    if "event" in message:
                        script.write(
                            json.dumps(
                                {"at": "t+%d" % int((time.time() - t_start) * 1000),
                                 "inject": spill({k: v for k, v in message.items()
                                                  if k != args.id_key})},
                                separators=(",", ":"),
                            )
                            + "\n"
                        )
                        script.flush()
                    continue
                after_ms = int((time.time() - entry["t0"]) * 1000)
                if "output" in message:
                    entry["emits"].append({"after_ms": after_ms,
                                           "output": spill(message["output"])})
                elif "input_request" in message:
                    entry["emits"].append({"after_ms": after_ms,
                                           "input_request": spill(message["input_request"])})
                else:
                    entry["reply"] = message
                    write_rule(entry)
                    pending.pop(mid, None)
                    if mid in order:
                        order.remove(mid)

    t_start = time.time()
    reader = threading.Thread(target=pump_out, daemon=True)
    reader.start()

    for raw in sys.stdin.buffer:
        child.stdin.write(raw)
        child.stdin.flush()
        try:
            request = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8").strip())
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            continue
        if isinstance(request, dict) and args.method_key in request:
            with lock:
                mid = request.get(args.id_key)
                pending[mid] = {"request": request, "t0": time.time(), "emits": []}
                order.append(mid)

    child.stdin.close()
    child.wait()
    script.close()
    return child.returncode


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

def main(argv):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=False)
    parser.add_argument("--script", action="append", default=[])
    parser.add_argument("--fault", default=None)
    parser.add_argument("--log", default=None)
    parser.add_argument("--record", default=None)
    parser.add_argument("--id-key", default="id")
    parser.add_argument("--method-key", default="method")
    parser.add_argument("--params-key", default="params")
    parser.add_argument("--unmatched", default="error",
                        choices=("error", "empty", "never"))
    parser.add_argument("--linger-ms", type=int, default=2000)
    parser.add_argument("--help", action="store_true")
    # Everything the package under test appends for its real backend
    # (--url, --token, --insecure, ...) lands here and is discarded.
    args, ignored = parser.parse_known_args(argv)

    if args.help:
        sys.stdout.write(__doc__)
        return 0

    log = open(args.log, "a", encoding="utf-8") if args.log else None
    if log:
        log.write("=== eh-fake-bridge %s (ignored: %s)\n" % (argv, ignored))
        log.flush()

    if args.record is not None:
        if "--" not in ignored:
            sys.stderr.write("eh-fake-bridge: --record needs `-- REAL-COMMAND ...'\n")
            return 2
        command = ignored[ignored.index("--") + 1 :]
        if not command:
            sys.stderr.write("eh-fake-bridge: --record needs a command after `--'\n")
            return 2
        return record(args.record, command, args)

    if not args.script:
        sys.stderr.write("eh-fake-bridge: nothing to do -- pass --script FILE.jsonl\n")
        return 2

    try:
        rules, injections = load_scripts(args.script)
    except (ScriptError, OSError) as err:
        sys.stderr.write("eh-fake-bridge: %s\n" % err)
        return 2

    if args.fault == "stderr-noise":
        threading.Thread(target=stderr_noise, daemon=True).start()

    writer = Writer(sys.stdout, log)
    try:
        Bridge(args, rules, injections, writer).run()
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        return 0
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
